Date: Tuesday, 13 July 1993 23:30:49 PST8
From: "James A. Donald" <jamesdon@infoserv.com>
Subject: hand waving on nanobearings

In <9307140125.AA07327@netcom.netcom.com>, drexler@netcom.com (K. Eric Drexler)

In short, Drexler argues that large complicated irregular
objects (nanomachines) can be arranged so that variations
in the potential cancel out almost perfectly.  

Clearly his argument is true for small highly symmetric
objects, such as isolated bearings.

I argue that the truth of this proposition for small highly
symmetric objects does not imply the truth of this
proposition for the large complex asymmetric objects one gets
by tightly locking such small objects together.

> Your use of "likely to be" in this context suggests that engineering
> systems are given at random, when in fact they result from more-or-less
> intelligent design. The analysis in Nanosystems (Section 10.3.6, "Coupled
> sites", and Section 10.8, "Barriers in extended systems") indicates that in
> well-designed systems, stiff coupling can be used to *reduce* barriers. You
> assume that the result will "likely" be the opposite.

Indeed it can be so used.  But each cancellation gives the
engineer one less degree of freedom to play with.  You can
run out of degrees of freedom very fast.  You cannot cancel 
variations with different spatial frequencies, and the larger 
the object the more spatial frequencies.

> You are proposing to argue about bearings by scaling from systems that lack
> the features of good bearings. This is silly. Random imperfections
> certainly ruin the bearings we've modeled.
> 
> >A good analog of a nanoscale proposed bearing is a macro
> >bearing lubricated by hot HOPG in a vacuum.
> 
> Why is this a good analog? I see negligible similarity.


HOPG is Highly Oriented Pyrolytic Graphite.  The planes
are parallel to the surface of the sheet of HOPG, but
randomly rotated with respect to nearby planes.  By your
model, the HOPG shear strength comes only from
imperfections, so the better grades of HOPG should be very
weak against shear force.  Yet HOPG seems reasonably
resistant to shear forces - it is quite solid.

This seems to indicate that there is something seriously
oversimplified in your model of how non uniformities in the
potential (static friction) arise.

.......

> > The point is that asymmetries cannot easily be shielded
> > out, that everything tends to couple to everything else,
> > that high spatial frequency forces couple to low spatial
> > frequency forces (harder to shield) through nonlinear
> > effects, that the micro world is messier than the
> > macroworld

........

> To get beyond hand waving, try modeling a bearing with 19
> atoms in the inner ring, 27 atoms in the outer ring, and a
> coaxial, coplanar geometry corresponding to the case in
> Fig. 10.14, p. 289. (Remember, this is a test of
> sensitivity to the pairwise interaction approximation, not
> to the effects of environmentally-imposed asymmetries,
> hence simplicity is appropriate.)


Obviously, if the inner and outer bearings have high
symmetries, and their symmetries are mutually prime, then
the energy barriers will be negligible, for bearings that
are small enough that their atoms do not shift
significantly under the mutual interaction. 

The point about three atom effects is that they make it
hard to shield out environmentally induced asymmetries,
hence my argument about nested carbon nanotubes lying on a
graphite surface.

......

> >One solution is to make the entire axle shaft one large
> >bearing.  Thus instead of dealing with a twenty atom
> >bearing, ten atoms in circumference and two atoms thick,
> >we are dealing with a thousand atom bearing, ten atoms in
> >circumference and one hundred atoms long.
> >
> >I think you will agree that such a large bearing is likely
> >to lock up solidly.
> 
> No, I would disagree because I understand nanomachine design well enough to
> know how to design structures of this sort that will turn smoothly.
> 
> >The total van der Waals potential is
> >so very large that any small angular variation, inevitable
> >when the circumference is only ten atoms and the rest of
> >the nearby machine is not cylindrically symmetric, is going
> >to be vastly larger than kT.
> 
> This is superficially plausible, but false.

Why is it false?  Remember we are no longer talking about
a small bearing with perfect symmetry, but instead a large
bearing with imperfect symmetry.  Precise cancellation will
require amazingly precise modeling of the forces
(intellectual obstacle), and will require fiddling various
subtle high order effects (physical obstacle and
intellectual obstacle).  The larger the bearing, the more
things there are that have to be canceled more precisely,
for example when you cancel the unwanted asymmetry in one
region by a deliberate asymmetry in another region, the
shaft will twist somewhat between the two regions, and this
twist will depend on the shaft angle.

In a large object, precisely canceling the nonuniformities
in the potential is going to become vastly more complex
than simply plugging perfect parts together.  You are going
to have to make a perfect whole from parts that become
imperfect when in close contact with each other.

> >One solution is to make the entire axle shaft one large
> >bearing....
> >...giving it a
> >larger circumference to match its long length, make it one
> >hundred atoms in circumference instead of ten....
> >An axle that
> >large will suffer local flexing of an Angstrom or so under
> >van der Waals forces, destroying the cancellation, and so
> >it will stick Glad Wrap fashion, in the manner that Hal
> >Finney describes.
> 
> Ah! You seem to assume that the interaction between the adjacent surfaces
> of shaft and sleeve is attractive and unstable. 


No.

I mean that if we make the shaft too large, atoms in the
shaft and the housing will move parallel to each other, as
the atoms in glad wrap presumably move, to locally match
spacing.

(Problem described by Hal Finney)

Such local matching will destroy cancellation.  The spatial
frequency of the potential will still be very high, but
the amplitude of the potential variations will not be
reduced by the high spatial frequency.

The rigidity required to prevent this local match up
problem increases with the size of the object.  A typical
nanocomponent is too small for this problem to be
important, but put a lot of large nanocomponents together,
and this will certainly happen.

............

> >When you rigidly lock a large number of such components
> >together you get a small macroscopic object, highly
> >asymmetric.  Some of the imperfections will randomly cancel
> >out.  Some will systematically cancel out.  Some will
> >randomly add due asymmetry,
> 
> Randomly adding? Sloppy design.


If, for example, one component has a periodic variation
with respect to rotation of period five in its potential,
and the other has period four, then when you put them
together there must be a phase for which the two
variations in potential add.

The same principle applies to any frequency component of
the potential with respect to motion.

Thus to avoid random additions places extreme restraints
on the engineer.  He has to ensure that all variations
have the corresponding spectrum, which is impossible except in
systems with very high symmetry.

> 
> >and some will systematically
> >add, due to large scale orderly asymmetry.
> 
> Systematically adding? Dreadful design! Fire the engineer!

1.  It may be impossible to avoid systematic addition,
because the larger and more irregular the system the more
interactions you have to cancel out, and the more precisely
you have to cancel them out.

2.  Even if it is possible in principle to cancel them
precisely, that 20% inaccuracy you mentioned is way
too poor to permit such cancellation.  You cannot model the
system accurately enough to evaluate what you need to
cancel.

Existing nanotechnology is the rational design of drugs.
Typically the designers are designing a molecule to fit
perfectly in a receptor on a virus or in a bacterium and
stay put in the receptor.

First they take a molecule that does a fair fit.  Then
generate a crystal of the receptor and plug, and do X ray
crystallography on it.  It is usually a poor fit.   Then
they use molecular modeling to design a molecule that
gives a better fit.  Then they do X ray crystallography again.

The amount of X ray crystallography that they need to do
gives me no confidence in the ability of molecular
modeling to precisely predict the shape and the forces,
and we will need extraordinarily precise predictions in
order to design in precise cancellations, assuming that
they can be designed in, and assuming that intellectual
effort required to find structures that yield perfect
cancellations is not intolerably great.

> 
> Donald asserts that asymmetries will produce large energy barriers in
> (necessarily asymmetrical) nanomachines, and appears to believe that the
> case for nanomachines rests on unrealistic symmetry assumptions. However,
> he shows no awareness of the analysis of irregular structures in sliding
> contact with regular structures (Nanosystems, Section 10.3), or of the
> analysis of the effects of load on otherwise symmetrical bearings (Section
> 10.4.4), or of the use of deliberate asymmetries to compensate for load
> (Section 10.4.8), or of the use of deliberate structural irregularities to
> compensate for edge effects in sliding rods and screws (Section 10.5.1,
> 10.5.2), nor of the use of tuning components to smooth the potential energy
> function of irregular objects sliding over irregular surfaces (Section
> 10.8.1). By assuming that *random* addition of energy barriers will be
> fatal to nanomachines of the sort proposed, he is in effect assuming that
> engineers will design systems *randomly*, giving no heed to key engineering
> constraints. This is not a useful basis for analyzing future technologies.


You can slide one large irregular object over another
irregular object if and only if there is no overlap in
spatial frequencies.  This seems difficult or impossible
to achieve, particularly as high order effects will produce
sum and difference frequencies.  The sum frequencies will
have insignificant amplitude and will usually be
incommensurate anyway, but the low difference frequencies
will lock the system up solid, as they presumably do in
HOPG, to judge from the moire patterns visible in tunneling
microscope images.

(Incidentally, in my previous posting where moire came out
moiri, this was because I used a tilde on the e, which the
mailer could not handle.)

The larger, and more irregular the nanomachine, the tighter
the engineering constraints needed to avoid intolerable
friction, assuming that the components are rigidly
connected in order to preserve predictability and
determinism.  Small complicated high order effects have
to be dealt with, so you get more constraints. Eventually
you are faced with a balancing act beyond what can be
predicted or controlled.

I am inclined to believe that control will fail before
prediction, because the micro world is messy.  You are
inclined to believe control will not fail for reasonable
sized nanomachines, because the micro world is uniform and
orderly.

Regardless of whether control will fail or not, prediction
will certainly fail, for precise cancellation, of order kT
in large irregular objects, requires precise prediction,
of order kT in large irregular objects, and molecular
modeling does not give us that at present, and there are
large problems in radically improving the accuracy of
molecular modeling.

> 
> If I can be excused for pointing at Nanosystems one more time:
> 
> "_Criticism of criticism_"
> 
> "Research in molecular nanotechnology requires a design perspective because
> it aims to describe workable systems. It is easy to describe unworkable
> systems, and criticisms of a critic's own bad design have on occasion been
> presented as if they were criticisms of molecular nanotechnology as a
> whole."
> 
> (From the preface, p.xviii)


It is easy to propose a highly symmetric nanocomponent that
will work perfectly, and can be proved to work perfectly
so long as it floats uselessly in isolation. In describing
large irregular complex objects that will work perfectly,
free from static friction, Drexler waves his hands as much
as I do, but whereas I wave my hands to point at
complexity, he waves complexity away.


