Subject: FYI : Black Hole

Some lighter fare for science and astronomy buffs:

------------------------------------------------------------
SCIENTISTS FIND NEW EVIDENCE OF BLACK HOLES IN SPACE

    LONDON (Reuter) - Astronomers have detailed fresh evidence
to prove the existence of black holes in space, one of the
universe's most intriguing phenomena.
    Writing in Thursday's edition of the science journal Nature,
U.S. scientist Walter Jaffe said his team had taken pictures of
a so-called nuclear accretion disk, which scientists have long
theorized surrounds a black hole.
    The existence of black holes has never been proved, but it
is believed they are stars so heavy that they have collapsed.
They produce so much gravity that everything within reach,
including light, is sucked into the center.
    The idea is that matter swirls around the black hole as it
is being pulled in. This would form an accretion disk, a flat
ellipse resembling a round Danish pastry.
    ``Using the planetary camera on the Hubble Space Telescope,
we have discovered an unexpectedly large disk of cool dust and
gas surrounding a bright unresolved nucleus in the active galaxy
NGC4261,'' wrote Jaffe, of Leiden University in the Netherlands.
    The article describes how the camera took pictures of a
galaxy in the constellation Virgo, about 40 million light-years
away. A light-year is the distance light, moving at 186,000
miles a second, can travel in a year.
    ``Clearly visible in the center of the image...is a sharply
defined absorbing disk that blocks light from the stars behind
it. At the very center of the disk is a bright point-like
nucleus.''
    It is probably an ``outer accretion disk,'' Jaffe said.
``The outer disk supplies fuel by way of the inner disk to the
central engine, probably a massive black hole,'' he wrote.
    Jaffe worked with Holland Ford and Laura Ferrarese of Johns
Hopkins University, Frank van den Bosch of Leiden and Robert
O'Connell of the University of Virginia.
    He said in a telephone interview that the object was first
seen about a year ago and the U.S. space agency NASA released
initial findings in November.
    He said observations made from land-based telescopes since
then have shown the disk is very active. ``They actually show
the material is circulating around the center. It seems to be
quite complicated; there is cold material and hot, ionized
material,'' Jaffe told Reuters.

