less than 5 cents per page.

>You are going
>to have to make a perfect whole from parts that become
>imperfect when in close contact with each other.

When did perfection in this sense become part of the product specification? 

>I mean that if we make the shaft too large, atoms in the
>shaft and the housing will move parallel to each other, as
>the atoms in glad wrap presumably move, to locally match
>spacing.

Excuse me -- Glad wrap?

>Such local matching will destroy cancellation.  The spatial
>frequency of the potential will still be very high, but
>the amplitude of the potential variations will not be
>reduced by the high spatial frequency.

As I mentioned earlier, this is discussed in Nanosystems as a way of making
a different and useful class of devices. It won't happen in properly
designed bearings. (Don't ask why here, go do your homework.)

>The rigidity required to prevent this local match up
>problem increases with the size of the object.

Wrong. Read the literature.

>If, for example, one component has a periodic variation
>with respect to rotation of period five in its potential,
>and the other has period four, then when you put them
>together there must be a phase for which the two
>variations in potential add.
>
>The same principle applies to any frequency component of
>the potential with respect to motion.
>
>Thus to avoid random additions places extreme restraints
>on the engineer.  He has to ensure that all variations
>have the corresponding spectrum, which is impossible except in
>systems with very high symmetry.

This isn't random, but highly predictable. Why not read the literature and
think about how to design so as to meet obvious constraints? From your
general approach, I'll wager that your background is in science, not
engineering.

>1.  It may be impossible to avoid systematic addition,
>because the larger and more irregular the system the more
>interactions you have to cancel out, and the more precisely
>you have to cancel them out.

How precisely do you think they have to be cancelled out, in order to make
a power-driven machine operate with smooth motions? (Before attempting to
inform the world of the answer to this, try reading the literature and
understanding the engineering objectives and constraints.)

>2.  Even if it is possible in principle to cancel them
>precisely, that 20% inaccuracy you mentioned is way
>too poor to permit such cancellation.  You cannot model the
>system accurately enough to evaluate what you need to
>cancel.

(Precisely?) Equating inaccurate knowledge of potentials with an inability
to design systems with good cancellation suggests profound mathematical
naivete, or at least a lack of thought. Even if one were to assume this, it
would make little difference. Molecular models can tell us what *kinds* of
systems can be made to work, and experimentation can later give us results
with no modeling approximations. Most of engineering progress has been
achieved by testing, fiddling, and correcting. To insist that existing
models must be perfectly predictive is silly.

>Existing nanotechnology is the rational design of drugs....

That's odd. I didn't hear this topic mentioned at the conference on
Nanotechnology in Tokyo last year.

>> ....the use of tuning components to smooth the potential energy
>> function of irregular objects sliding over irregular surfaces (Section
>> 10.8.1). [of Nanosystems]
>
>You can slide one large irregular object over another
>irregular object if and only if there is no overlap in
>spatial frequencies. This seems difficult or impossible
>to achieve, particularly as high order effects will produce
>sum and difference frequencies....

This is quite confused. You show no sign of knowing the engineering
constraints, haven't mentioned an engineering application, haven't tried to
design a nanocomponent, don't respond to actual proposals, and haven't done
your homework. This particular response suggests that you've never looked
at the literature on this subject, even though you were given a reference
in the very paragraph you were responding to. Why do you think it
appropriate to pretend to knowledge that you don't have? Isn't that a gross
discourtesy to the readers of this mailing list, or like passing
counterfeit money?

>It is easy to propose a highly symmetric nanocomponent that
>will work perfectly, and can be proved to work perfectly
>so long as it floats uselessly in isolation. In describing
>large irregular complex objects that will work perfectly,
>free from static friction, Drexler waves his hands as much
>as I do, but whereas I wave my hands to point at
>complexity, he waves complexity away.

This (again) attacks a straw man. It is easy to warn of complexity but
takes real work to describe it and how to cope with it. That's why I wrote
a book and have been citing it rather than trying to reproduce it in this
exchange. Donald's criticism might have some force if he gave any sign of
knowing the state of the field.

Since Donald apparently objects to learning before attempting to teach, I
will again cite, for the interested reader, discussions of how to make the
energy barriers of irregular, asymmetrical systems meet real engineering
constraints (without achieving Donald's bogus goal of perfection):

>> ...he shows no awareness of the analysis of irregular structures in sliding
>> contact with regular structures (Nanosystems, Section 10.3), or of the
>> analysis of the effects of load on otherwise symmetrical bearings (Section
>> 10.4.4), or of the use of deliberate asymmetries to compensate for load
>> (Section 10.4.8), or of the use of deliberate structural irregularities to
>> compensate for edge effects in sliding rods and screws (Section 10.5.1,
>> 10.5.2), nor of the use of tuning components to smooth the potential energy
>> function of irregular objects sliding over irregular surfaces (Section
>> 10.8.1).

Note that the strategies discussed are local to subsystems, that
intermolecular forces are quite short range, and that the system design
problem can therefore be decomposed into adequately-decoupled subsystem
design problems. With good design practice, there need be no blowup of
complexity from this trivial source.

I began this exchange because two people in one day told me that Donald had
criticisms and sounded as though he knew what he was talking about. It is,
at this point, clear that he is able to sound knowledgeable when ignorant,
and confident when wrong. I assume that he will not lose this skill.

Further messages on this topic should be posted to sci.nanotech. I'm sure
that any interesting results will be posted back here.

Eric Drexler


