Let me point out several implicit assumptions which you are making here.

1) You assume that "entities" to be "invoked" can be arbitrarily selected
with results that can be readily predicted beforehand.  In other words, you
assume that Batman is equivalent to Avalokiteshvara is equivalent to Baron
Samedi.  Contrary to your statement above, Batman, Superman and Wonder
Woman do _not_ form part of our "cultural mythos", at least not in the way
that loa are part of the "cultural mythos" of Voudun or Santeria, for
instance.

2) You assume that the configuration and attributes of "entities" to be
"invoked" has no significance.  When visualizing Tibetan Buddhist _yidam_
(or meditational deities, which, by the way, are _not_ treated as having
"objective reality"), for instance, things such as the hand gestures,
dress, ornamentation, number of arms and faces, and the colors thereof are
_very_ strictly prescribed.  Some neurophysiologists investigating
Vajrayana meditation have suggested that these attributes may cause
specific effects or "resonances" within the nervous system.

3) You assume that ritual design is trivial, and that any ritual is as good
(i.e. efficacious) as any other. 

