Date: Wed, 14 Jul 1993 21:55:46 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: AIT: Configuration space changes under evolution

Derek Zahn:
> > As
> > population fitness increases, number of competitive genomes decreases.
> > Should actually be applied to entire ecologies since fitness landscapes
> > change with coevolution, but is too large to fit in the margin of this
> > email message.

Hal Finney:
> I'm really not sure that configuration space decreases
> under the influence of evolution.

I think Derek's saying that the space of fitter genomes shrinks,
not the configuration space itself.  I'd  agree, and put it
another way: the editing probability to configurations of greater fitness 
goes down as fitness increases (with exceptions, see below).  Editing
probability = f(delta-fitness/editing distance).

>[More diversity, functionality, etc. as life evolves]

We can look on flying, swimming, walking, etc. as connected sub-spaces 
(niches) inside an infinite space (which includes all possible phenotypes, 
all possible memes, etc.)  Any particular subspace becomes exhausted,
but the search space in general is open-ended (much larger than
any of the subspaces, and the subspaces are only sparsely connected, so 
that the editing probability between most regions is extremely low).
Inside a niche the editing probability to a fitter configuration becomes
smaller and smaller, but in some cases the fitness increases _and_
the editing probability to higher fitness goes up, indicating 
a "quantum leap" into a new, fertile niche.  (Plug in Tim May's
favorite k-space example of Intel MOS -> DRAM, CPUs, etc. here).

> I'm even wondering if a cyclic situation could develop, in which a population
> of A's could be invaded by a B, leading eventually to all B's; but then the
> B's could be replaced by C's, only to have the C's be replaced by A's...
> If such a thing could happen, it would challenge the notion that evolution
> inevitably leads to increased logical depth.

This probably does occur, and we can only say it's not logically
deep because we have recorded that A occured the first time.
More often, we might get A' which is only slightly different from A.
This might be logical depth, but of a trivial nature -- like a bunch
of computer programs trying to predict whether each other will
halt.  Computing Omega may be very difficult, but that doesn't
mean it's useful.  (Brings to mind one scenario of the future I've 
comtemplated: a society made up entirely of lawyers filing suits against 
each other, salesmen selling sales pitches to each other, etc. :-)

For both these reasons, the argument "function A took billions of
years to evolve, therefore it's logically deep, therefore it's
to hard for humans to build anytime soon" is bogus.  (Also consider
we've already knocked off superior versions of walking,
swimming, flying, photosynthesis, echolocation, etc. and things
like nuclear power which nature doesn't seem to have arrived
at). 

