Date: Wed, 14 Jul 1993 23:02:32 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: General Assemblers

The acronym "GA" is already taken (genetic algorithms, a common
topic here).  Let's stick to "assemblers", with the prefix "nano-" 
if necessary?

Self-reproducing things, in this case living, make up about
(wave hands) 20% of the world economy right now (food, lumber, drugs),
somewhat higher if we consider the utility of the most 
basic 20% (necessities) to be higher than the most expendable 20% 
(luxuries).  Will nanotech bias things towards more
than 20% self-rep, or will it continue the trend of the industrial
revolution towards more nonlocal production?

In a world that is political as well as economic, local control
becomes a value to be thrown in the pot along with economies
of scale, etc. that favor nonlocal manufacturing.
To give one example, photovoltaics are more popular than nuclear
power despite the latter being far more economical.

It's quite possible that nanotech does not change the economic balance,
that it will improve nonlocal manufacturing as fast as it improves
self-rep (and for the same reasons, the million-fold increase
in assembly frequency).  Even so, as manufacturing becomes more
efficient it (like agriculture before it) decreases as a percentage
of economic output.  ("Services" increase, especially medicine,
given the coming huge increase in life expectancy variance).

As with agriculture, more manufacturing will be done for enjoyment
(backyard gardens, yuppie part-time farmers, etc.) and uneconomic
political power reasons (farm subsidies to minimize dependence on
foreign food).  Local, self-contained manufacturing will be 
increasingly favored due to hobby enjoyment and/or political 
self-sufficiency reasons, even if there is no scale advantage.  The 
U.S. military's concern over industrial self-suffiency played a big 
role in the forming of Sematech, the subsidy of the U.S. launch industry,
etc.  But it isn't just statists who desire such self-sufficiency;
remote colonies from Oceania to space are a common desire that
many libertarians are willing to pay a premium for.  The current
premium is far too high, but if manufactured goods become a smaller
part of the family budget, the premium goes down even if the relative
cost of local/nonlocal production hasn't changed.

Self-rep also has an important niche where there is a physical 
bottleneck.  For example, launch costs to space are high, but once 
out there we have trillions of tons of nanotech-food to play with.
Self-rep, (or to put it more precisely and usefully, an expanding 
autocatalytic set) will be necessary to grow the industrial infrastructure 
from "seeds".  The self-rep needs to be very tight, closing all the loops, 
so that very little mass in "vitamins" is needed to develop a
very large, highly productive space industry.  (Exponential growth
is soon dominated by the need for even tiny fractions of vitamin
parts; that's why details are important!)

Just as important is the ability of these machines
to manufacture a wide variety of desired products: highly
agile manufacturing, where new designs are beamed up to the
assemblers and "printed out".  Assemblers, 3D printers, and
similar highly agile tech serve as the blacksmith's forge of space 
colonization.

For many problems self-rep is not a useful approach.  Atom-by-atom
control itself is more important for problems like brain enhancement, 
cryonics, uploading, etc.   High frequency operations (assemblies/second,
features/second, etc.) are usually important.   The relevant
scientific knowledge base (in many cases, neurology) is 
usually important.  Self-rep is a useful tool that can
be brought to bear on a few interesting problems; it is not
a panacea.

